rly, W1: could help lower chows.\n\nT9: same.\n\nB8: B8 is in the middle. B7-B8-B9 possible. B7: 3 left, B9: 2 \u2014 possible.\n\nBut B8 only one in hand.\n\nNow, look at history: I played B3, B2, B5, etc. \u2014 dumping B middle.\n\nBut I have a B6 pung \u2014 so committed to B suit.\n\nStill, B8 doesn't help much.\n\nNow, note: I have T7 in hand, and I already used one T7 in the chow [T6,T7,T8]. Since there are only 4 T7s, and I used one in chow and have one in hand, that's two accounted for. Two left unshown \u2014 so possible others have them.\n\nBut T7 is part of a common chow \u2014 so playing it might be dangerous.\n\nHowever, given that [T6,T7,T8] is already formed, and I'm not building another, and no one has shown interest in T7, perhaps it's acceptable.\n\nBut compare to W1: W1 is terminal, but W2 and W3 are abundant. Someone could be doing Lower Tiles or Full Flush in W.\n\nBut W5 only 2 left \u2014 maybe wall is thin.\n\nAnother idea: Seat Wind is South (F2), Prevailing is West (F3).\n\nI played F1 twice, F4 once, F3 once \u2014 so I've discarded F1, F4, F3. F2 (South) \u2014 I haven't discarded it. And F2 has 4 unshown \u2014 full set. So F2 is safe for me to keep, but I don\u2019t have it.\n\nFor others: someone might want Seat Wind or Prevalent Wind.\n\nBut I have no F2.\n\nJ2 is Green Dragon \u2014 neutral.\n\nNow, best discard: let's see which tile gives me the most flexibility or safety.\n\nOption: Play J2 \u2014 get rid of a singleton honor that might be dangerous, and I\u2019m not building dragons.\n\nBut if I keep J2, I could draw another and make a pair, or even pung later.\n\nBut opportunities are low.\n\nAlternatively, T9: terminal, and T9 is in [T6,T7,T8] vicinity, but not helping.\n\nNote: I have T7 and T9 in hand \u2014 gap at T8.\n\nUnlikely.\n\nB8: similar.\n\nBut here's a key point: the only tile I have that is duplicated in my hand across melds and hand is... none really.\n\nWait: I have W6 and W7 \u2014 could go for W6-W7-W8.\n\nW8 has 3 unshown \u2014 still possible.\n\nW9: could go for W7-W8-W9 \u2014 same.\n\nBut W8 is critical.\n\nW1 is far away.\n\nSo W1 is the least connected to any potential sequence.\n\nMoreover, W1 is a terminal \u2014 less valuable than middle tiles.\n\nIn fact, in standard strategy, terminals and honors are discarded first when cleaning up.\n\nI have already discarded W5, F1, F4, etc.\n\nSo W1 is a candidate.\n\nBut is it safe?\n\nW1: 4 unshown \u2014 none discarded visibly in histories except possibly indirectly.\n\nCheck: did anyone discard W1?\n\nMy history: no.\n\nNext: no.\n\nAcross: no.\n\nPrevious: no.\n\nSo no W1 discarded yet \u2014 unusual? Or maybe not.\n\nUnshown says W1:4 \u2014 so all 4 still in play. That means no W1 has been discarded or used in revealed melds.\n\nBut I have one in hand \u2014 so 3 others still in wall or hands.\n\nBut no one has discarded it \u2014 could mean someone is protecting it, e.g., doing All Terminals or Nine Gates.\n\nBut Nine Gates in W would require 1112345678999 \u2014 but I have W6,W7,W9 \u2014 not matching.\n\nAnd someone would have to have concealed it.\n\nLow probability.\n\nMore likely, W1 is safe-ish.\n\nBut compared to J2 \u2014 J2 has never been discarded either.\n\nJ2: 4 unshown \u2014 I have one, so 3 others out there.\n\nJ3 was punged \u2014 so someone has a kong or pung.\n\nJ1: 2 unshown \u2014 I played one, someone else played one? My history: Play J1, across: no, next: no, previous: Play J1 \u2014 so two J1s discarded. So 2 left.\n\nSo J1 is half gone.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nPerhaps the safest discard is J2, because:\n\n- It's a dragon, but I'm not using it.\n- Dragons are often punged early \u2014 but J2 hasn't been.\n- J3 was punged, so maybe that player has only one dragon.\n- But still risky.\n\nAlternatively, T9: T9 has been discarded by across and me \u2014 so at least two out. 3 unshown originally, minus two, so one left? Unshown says T9:3 \u2014 I have one, so 2 others should be out \u2014 yes, across played T9, I played T9 \u2014 so two discarded. So 3 - 2 = 1 left in wall.\n\nSo T9 is rare.\n\nPlaying T9 might be safe because only one left \u2014 but if someone is waiting on T9, it's dangerous.\n\nSame for W1: no W1 discarded \u2014 so all 4 are live \u2014 more dangerous.\n\nGeneral rule: tiles that have been discarded are safer.\n\nT9 has been discarded by me and across \u2014 so at least two instances \u2014 so relatively safe to play again.\n\nSimilarly, I played T7 before? No \u2014 I Chi\u2019d T7, meaning I used a discarded T7 to form chow \u2014 so someone else discarded T7.\n\nWho? The history says: \"Chi T7\" \u2014 and Chi can only be done on the immediate predecessor's discard.\n\nSo the previous player must have discarded T7.\n\nLook: previous player history: ... Play T3, Play B5, Play T6, Peng J3, Play T2 \u2014 no T7.\n\nWait, inconsistency?\n\nMy history: Play F4, Play B2, Peng B6, Play W5, Play T1, Play T3, Play J1, Play F1, Play F1, Chi T7, Play B3.\n\nSo after Play F1, I did Chi T7 \u2014 so the previous player must have discarded T7 just before.\n\nBut their history ends with Play T2 \u2014 but that's their last action.\n\nThe order is: previous player -> me -> next -> across.\n\nSo when I took an action, the player before me (previous) made a discard, which I claimed with Chi.\n\nSo their discard of T7 is not listed in their history under \"Play\", because it's the most recent action, perhaps not logged yet in the provided snippet.\n\nYes, logically, the previous player must have discarded T7 for me to Chi it.\n\nTherefore, T7 has been discarded \u2014 so at least one T7 is out.\n\nI used one in chow, have one in hand \u2014 so three accounted: one discarded, one in my chow, one in my hand. Only one left.\n\nSo T7 is very rare.\n\nTherefore, playing T7 now would be extremely safe \u2014 only one left, and already one exposed.\n\nWhereas other players may avoid it.\n\nSo T7 is a safe discard.\n\nSimilarly, T6: I have in chow, previous player played T6 \u2014 so at least one discarded.\n\nT6: unshown 3 \u2014 4 total, one in my chow, one discarded \u2014 so 2 left.\n\nOkay.\n\nSo between T7 and other options, T7 is the safest.\n\nAlso, T7 in hand is redundant \u2014 I already have a chow using T7, and no way to use a second T7 constructively.\n\nWhereas W6, W7 could form a chow if I draw W8.\n\nJ2 could become useful.\n\nT9 could be part of T7-T8-T9, but I have T7, not T8.\n\nSo T7 is less useful than T9 even.\n\nWait: I have T7 and T9 \u2014 if I had T8, I could make T7-T8-T9 chow.\n\nBut I already have T6-T7-T8 \u2014 conflict on T7 and T8.\n\nCannot use one tile in two melds.\n\nSo impossible to make both chows.\n\nTherefore, having T7 in hand is wasteful.\n\nWhereas T9 is not used anywhere \u2014 so slightly more flexible.\n\nBut still, T9 is terminal.\n\nBut T7 is safer to discard.\n\nMoreover, discarding T7 gives up least potential.\n\nSo best move: Play T7.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
