ns T8 is fully exposed. In fact, my own chow [T6,T7,T8] uses one T8, and perhaps others discarded. T9 can only form a chow with T7-T8, but T8 is gone. So T9 cannot form a chow anymore. Thus, T9 is useless for chows. It's a terminal, so only useful as part of a terminal/honor pung or pair.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have other T9s.\n\nSo T9 is dead tile \u2014 cannot contribute to any chow, and pung unlikely.\n\nSimilarly, **W5**: W5 has only one left. W6 is 0 \u2014 all gone (used in [W6,W6,W6]). W4: 1 left. So W4-W5-W6 chow impossible. W5 could only be used in W5-W5-W5 pung, but I only have one. Unlikely.\n\nSo W5 is also nearly dead.\n\nNow between F2, J1, J2 \u2014 all honors, any could potentially form pungs.\n\nBut F2 is my **Seat Wind**, so if I ever get a pung, it scores 2 points. Same as Dragon Pung.\n\nBut **F2 is safer to keep** than dragons because:\n- Dragons are universal scoring (2 points each), while seat wind is personal.\n- However, seat wind pung is same value.\n\nBut risk: Discarding **J1** or **J2** might feed an opponent\u2019s dragon pung.\n\nLooking at unshown:\n- J1: 1 left \u2014 so three already out. Who has them?\n- My hand: 1 J1\n- Previous player: Peng J3, and discarded J1 three times \u2014 so they discarded three J1s? History: Play J1, Play J1, Play J1 \u2014 yes, three times.\n- That means they discarded three J1s \u2014 so they had at least three, now discarded.\n- Standard: 4 copies. If they discarded three, and I have one, then all four are accounted for.\n- Therefore, **no J1 left** \u2014 unshown says J1:1 \u2014 contradiction?\n\nWait: \"Unshown tiles are: [J1: 1; ...]\" \u2014 unshown means not revealed on table, i.e., still in wall or in hands.\n\nBut previous player discarded J1 three times \u2014 that\u2019s three on table.\nI have one in hand.\nTotal: 4.\nSo J1 should have 0 unshown \u2014 but system says J1:1 unshown.\n\nPossibly error, or one in wall.\n\nBut if three discarded and I hold one, total four \u2014 so unshown should be 0.\n\nUnless \u201cunshown\u201d excludes hand tiles?\n\nNo \u2014 unshown typically means not visible to you, including opponents\u2019 hands and wall.\n\nBut you know your own hand.\n\nSo unshown count should exclude your own tiles.\n\nYes \u2014 logical.\n\nSo total J1: 4 copies.\nDiscarded by previous: 3 (in history: Play J1 three times)\nI hold 1.\nSo all 4 are accounted for \u2014 none in wall.\nThus unshown should be 0 \u2014 but stated as 1.\n\nPossibly typo, or I miscount.\n\nCheck history: \"Play J1\" appears three times in previous player\u2019s history \u2014 yes.\nAnd I have one J1 in hand.\nSo 3+1=4 \u2014 no J1 left.\n\nTherefore, **J1 is safe to discard** \u2014 cannot complete any pung anymore.\n\nSimilarly, **J2**: I have one, unshown: 2 \u2014 so two others exist.\n\nPrevious player discarded J2 once \u2014 so at least one out.\n\nTotal: 4.\nOne discarded (by previous), one in my hand \u2014 so two unshown (in wall or others' hands).\n\nSo J2 still possible for pung.\n\nThus, **J1 is completely safe** to discard \u2014 no one can use it to complete a pung.\n\nSimilarly, **F2**: I have one, unshown: 4 \u2014 so all four still available? But F2 is South, my seat wind.\n\nOthers may want it.\n\nBut no one has exposed wind melds.\n\nF1: 0 unshown \u2014 all gone (I played twice, across played twice \u2014 total four).\nF3: 0 \u2014 previous played multiple times.\nF4: 0 \u2014 multiple plays.\n\nSo only F2 remains unexposed.\n\nThus, F2 is relatively safe \u2014 but still, someone might be collecting.\n\nBut since no one has Peng F2, and F2 is my seat wind, likely others are not collecting.\n\nStill, safer than J2.\n\nNow, **T9**: T9 has two unshown.\nT8: 0 \u2014 all gone.\nSo T9 cannot form a chow \u2014 only possible in pung or pair.\nBut no one has T9 meld, and I have one \u2014 so discarding T9 is low risk.\n\nSimilarly, **W5**: W5:1 unshown, W6:0, W4:1 \u2014 W4-W5-W6 not possible. W5-W5-W5 possible but unlikely.\n\nSo both T9 and W5 are dead tiles.\n\nAmong the options:\n- **Play J1**: Safest \u2014 all four accounted for, cannot help anyone.\n- **Play T9**: Safe \u2014 terminals often discarded late.\n- **Play W5**: Also dead tile.\n- **Play F2**: My seat wind \u2014 better to keep for potential 2 points.\n- **Play J2**: Riskier \u2014 two left, could complete a dragon pung.\n\nBest move: discard a harmless tile.\n\nJ1 is most harmless \u2014 cannot be used by anyone.\n\nEven if someone is waiting for a pair, but pung requires three.\n\nWith three already discarded and I hold one, maximum one J1 can be in hand elsewhere \u2014 cannot form pung.\n\nSo **J1 is safe**.\n\nSimilarly, T9: two unshown \u2014 could someone have two T9s for a pair? Possible, but unlikely to win off a single T9 discard.\n\nBut J1 is safer.\n\nAlso, keeping F2 gives potential for seat wind score.\n\nKeeping J2 gives dragon option.\n\nBut we need to discard.\n\nFinal choice: **Play J1**\n\nIt has no future value for me (cannot form pung), and is safe.\n\nW5 and T9 are also candidates, but J1 is safer.\n\nWait \u2014 what about scoring?\n\nIf I keep F2 and later draw two more, I get 2 points for Seat Wind.\n\nSame as Dragon Pung.\n\nBut J1 is gone \u2014 no chance.\n\nSo better to keep F2 than J1.\n\nBut I have to discard one.\n\nSince J1 is useless and safe, discard it.\n\nSimilarly, T9 is useless.\n\nBut T9 is a terminal \u2014 could be part of Outside Hand (8 points) if all melds contain terminals or honors.\n\nLet\u2019s check:\n\nMy melds:\n- [B6,B6,B6]: B6 \u2014 middle tile, not terminal \u2014 so **not** Outside Hand material.\n- [T6,T7,T8]: T6,T7,T8 \u2014 T6 and T8 are not terminals (1 or 9), T8 is 8 \u2014 so not terminal.\n- [W7,W8,W9]: W9 is terminal, W7,W8 not.\n\nSo none of my melds are exclusively terminals or honors.\n\nThus, **Outside Hand** impossible.\n\nSimilarly, **All Simples** impossible because of W7,W8,W9 (W7,W8 not simple? Simples are 2-8, so W7,W8 ok, but W9 is terminal \u2014 so not all simples.\n\nAnyway, not relevant.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nBest discard: **J1**, because:\n- Cannot contribute to any winning pattern for opponents (all four accounted for).\n- Useless in my hand.\n- Safer than J2 or F2.\n\nT9 and W5 are also dead, but J1 is the safest honor to discard.\n\nReason: J1 (Red Dragon) is completely safe to discard as all four copies are accounted for (three discarded by the previous player and one in my hand), making it impossible for any opponent to complete a pung. It holds no value for my hand, whereas F2 (my Seat Wind) and J2 retain potential for scoring patterns. T9 and W5 are also poor keepers, but J1 is the safest discard.\n\nAnswer: Play J1"}
