te \u2014 you need one more meld or pair. You're one tile away from completing a standard four-meld-one-pair hand.\n\nLooking at available tiles and discards:\n- Many terminals and honors have been discarded.\n- You lack a pair. Any duplicate tile can become a pair.\n- F2 (South Wind) is valuable because it gives you a chance at **Seat Wind** (2 points), which contributes toward the required 8-point minimum. Since you already have a Seat Wind tile, discarding it removes any possibility of scoring that pattern unless you later claim another F2 \u2014 but F2s are still abundant (4 unseen), so maybe recoverable.\n\nHowever, **none of your current hand tiles form pairs**, and you must discard one.\n\nBest strategy: Minimize loss of potential scoring patterns.\n\nAmong options:\n- **F2** is the only tile tied to a specific 2-point pattern (**Seat Wind**) that cannot be achieved with any other tile.\n- All other discards (T2, T9, W5, J2) do not contribute uniquely to a pattern.\n\nThus, **keeping F2** preserves a potential scoring element.\n\nTherefore, do **not** discard F2.\n\nBetween T2, T9, W5, J2:\n- W5: Only 1 left unseen, and you\u2019ve already discarded it once \u2014 suggests it may be somewhat safe.\n- T9: Multiple players have discarded T9 \u2014 very likely safe.\n- T2: Also discarded by multiple players \u2014 also likely safe.\n- J2: Next player discarded it \u2014 likely safe.\n\nBut consider: You have no use for any of these singles. Prioritize safety and preserve slight pattern potential.\n\nJ2 could help for **Dragon Pung** (2 points) or **Two Dragon Pungs** (6 points) if drawn again, but chances are low.\n\nT2 is part of \"Reversible Tiles\", but you don\u2019t have enough such tiles to build that hand.\n\nW5 appears in few viable sequences.\n\nSince all discards are relatively safe, choose the one **least likely to help an opponent** and **least useful to you**.\n\nT9 is a terminal, often used in edge hands. But given that two T9s remain and multiple were discarded, likely not dangerous.\n\nBut note: You have **[T6, T7, T8]** as a meld. If you had T9, you could potentially have extended thinking around T7-T8-T9, but you already completed that meld \u2014 so T9 is irrelevant.\n\nSimilarly, W5: you have [W7, W8, W9] \u2014 W5 doesn't connect.\n\nSo none of the tiles in hand are useful beyond singletons.\n\nNow, final decision:\n\n**Discard T9** \u2014 it completes no chow in your hand, is a terminal (less flexible), and has been frequently discarded, indicating safety.\n\nAlternatively, **discard W5** \u2014 you already discarded it once, and it\u2019s less connected to your existing structures.\n\nBut wait: **You previously discarded W5** \u2014 meaning you chose to get rid of it earlier. Now you have another? No \u2014 looking back: \u201cYour hand tiles are: [W5: 1; ...]\u201d \u2014 so you drew a new W5 after discarding one.\n\nStill, W5 is not helping.\n\nBut among all, **F2 is most valuable to keep** due to **Seat Wind** (2 points), which helps reach 8 points.\n\nSo we must avoid discarding F2.\n\nSame logic applies: Keep J2 for possible dragon pung (2 points).\n\nKeep T2? Part of reversible set, but not enough support.\n\nBest discard: **T9**\n\nBecause:\n- It\u2019s a terminal.\n- Not part of any potential internal sequence in your hand.\n- Frequently discarded.\n- Doesn\u2019t contribute to Seat Wind, Prevalent Wind (West), or Dragons.\n- Safest in terms of pattern preservation.\n\nReason:  \nT9 is a terminal tile with limited value in current hand and has been frequently discarded by others, indicating low risk. Discarding it preserves potentially useful tiles like F2 (Seat Wind) and J2 (Dragon), both of which contribute to scoring patterns. Other options like W5 or T2 are slightly more flexible, but T9 offers the least strategic benefit to keep.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
